response:
Of course pentagons are five-sided, so the perimeter of the pentagon is $$|AP| + |DP| + |CP| + |BP| + |AB|.$$ Here "| |" denotes the length. Notice that $P$ is the midpoint of $BD$, so we have: \begin{align*} |DP| &= |AP| \\ |AB| &= |BC| \end{align*} We know that $P$ and $B$ lie on the same line, so we have: \begin{align*} |AP| + |DP| &= |AB| \\ |AB| &= 2|AP| = 2(4) = 8 \end{align*} The rest of this example is painful. Notice also that $P$ and $C$ lie along a line that is perpendicular to $BD$, so we have: \begin{align*} |CP| &= |BP| = 4 \end{align*} We also know that $\triangle BCP$ is a right triangle, so we have: \begin{align*} |BP| + |CP| &= 8 \end{align*} Finally, notice that $\triangle ABP$ is a right triangle, so we have: \begin{align*} |AB| + |AP| &= 12 & \angle A + \angle B= 90 &\angle C= 90 \end{align*}
